    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Postdoctoral-Researcher-in-Study-of-Religion/1373446957/": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Study of Religion",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher in Study of Religion Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Postdoctoral Researcher in Study of Religion The Centre of Excellence in Meliorist Philosophy of Suffering (MePhiS) , funded by the Research Council of Finland at the University of Helsinki and Tampere University, invites application for a Postdoctoral Researcher position in the Study of Religion . The position is open within MePhiS’s research team Future, Futurelessness and Vernacular Responses to Suffering . The position is fix-term for three years starting in January 2027 or earlier/later as agreed MePhiS engages in philosophical and interdisciplinary scholarship that places the concept of suffering at the centre of philosophical reflection and offers conceptual and argumentative tools for a deeper understanding of human engagement with affliction, starting from the meliorist (Lat. melior , better) idea that the world can and should be made better through active human efforts. According to meliorism, a positive outcome is neither guaranteed (optimism) nor impossible (pessimism). The team Future, Futurelessness, and Vernacular Responses to Suffering (WP5) explores vernacular religious and non-religious encounters and responses—such as explanations, justifications, and imaginations—related to suffering. We are particularly interested in both empirical and theoretical approaches that address human and non-human death, climate change, and the loss of nature amidst an increasingly unpredictable future. Our aim is to understand how different groups engage with contemporary (poly)crises and whether these encounters reflect a melioristic stance toward suffering. We are especially searching for expertise that complements our present team’s competences. The Postdoctoral Researcher will be responsible for pursuing the research aims described above in the context of the Study of Religion; authoring scholarly publications independently and collaboratively on these topics; collaborating with other members of the MePhiS, including through joint publications and other research activities; and contributing to teaching and administrative duties, amounting to approximately 5-10 % of the working time. Requirements for the position and assessment of qualifications We are looking for a candidate with expertise in the empirical and theoretical Study of Religion in contemporary society , and interested in advancing the research aims described above. Applicants must hold a doctoral degree in a relevant field within the humanities or social sciences (e.g. Religious Studies, Anthropology or Sociology of Religion) and have the ability to conduct independent scholarly work as well as the teaching skills necessary for the position. Other academic activities of MePhiS are expected. A pre-existing interest in the general theme of the CoE is an advantage. The working language of MePhiS is English, and excellent written and oral communication skills in English are required. When assessing applicants’ qualifications, attention will be paid to international academic activities, including high-quality peer-reviewed publications. Applicants should be prepared to provide proof of their qualifications upon request. Please do not submit AI-generated proposals. What we offer The position is fix-term for three years starting in January 2027 or earlier/later as agreed. A six-month trial period applies to new employees at University of Helsinki. The salary for the position will be based on level 5 of the job requirement scheme for teaching and research personnel in the salary system of Finnish universities. In addition, the appointee will be paid a salary component based on personal performance. The annual gross salary is €43,000 - €50,000. In Finnish universities, the teaching and research staff (app. 65% of total staff) have a special system regarding the free time equivalent to holiday. They belong to the 1,612 annual working hour system, in which the annual amount of paid holiday is app. 5-6 weeks on average per year. The appointee is expected to reside in Finland while employed by the University of Helsinki. The university is an internationally renowned research university offering a wide range of staff benefits, including occupational health care and health insurance, flexible working hours, excellent campus sports facilities, and opportunities for professional development. The University provides support for internationally recruited employees with their transition to work and life in Finland. More information here. The Faculty of Theology is well-known for its internationally acknowledged research in the fields represented by MePhiS, and there are close ties with the Faculty of Law, the Faculty of Humanities, the Faculty of Social Science, and the Faculty of Education, as well as other units on the City Centre Campus. The Faculty of Theology and the University of Helsinki welcomes applicants from a variety of genders, linguistic and cultural backgrounds, and minorities. A diverse and equitable study and work culture is important to us. That is why we do our best to promote an inclusive university community. We encourage all qualified applicants from diverse backgrounds to apply for our positions. Click this link to read about accessibility and inclusivity at our University. For more information about Helsinki, please see My Helsinki . For further information about Finland, please visit infoFinland and This is Finland . How to apply The application must be accompanied by the following documents in PDF format: CV (max. 3 pages, including contact details of two referees) A list of publications A letter of motivation (max. 1 page) outlining your motivation for applying, relevant expertise, and how you would contribute to the research aims and collaborative activities of the project. A research proposal (max. 3 pages) consisting of a description of how your previous research experience and current work align with the overarching aims of the team of Future, Futurelessness and Vernacular Responses to Suffering ; a research idea outlining why the project’s themes or approach interest you and how you position your own research within this context; a preliminary publication plan. Other attachments, including letters of recommendation or certificates, are not required at this point. Applications must be submitted through the University of Helsinki electronic recruitment system by clicking on the link below. Current employees of the University of Helsinki must submit their applications through SAP Fiori’s Suffeli recruitment portal. The closing date for applications is 1st of November 2026 (at 23:56/UTC+3). The online interviews to shortlisted candidates will be announced later. For further information about the position please contact the project PI, Professor Terhi Utriainen, terhi.utriainen(at)helsinki.fi. For questions regarding the application process, please contact recruitment(at)helsinki.fi (please indicate in the heading which position you are inquiring about). JOIN US TO BUILD A BETTER WORLD – TOGETHER! #HelsinkiUniCareers Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 1.11.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Theology Sub-unit: Faculty of Theology ID: 5238 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Teaching and research positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why Finland CAREER SITES Academic careers Expert careers FACULTIES AND UNITS Flamma intranet Faculties and Units © University of Helsinki 2022 × Cookie Consen