    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Doctoral-researcher-in-Food-Materials-Science%2C-MSCA-Doctoral-Network/1374476257/": {
      "title": "Doctoral researcher in Food Materials Science, MSCA Doctoral Network",
      "description": "Doctoral researcher in Food Materials Science, MSCA Doctoral Network Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Doctoral researcher in Food Materials Science, MSCA Doctoral Network Doctoral researcher in Food Materials Science: Aerogels for Oral Lubrication - part of a MSCA Doctoral Network on FAroGels - Food-Grade Aerogels for Sustainable Food Systems Department of Food and Nutrition of the Faculty of Agriculture and Forestry, University of Helsinki, Finalnd, invites applications for a Doctoral researcher, FAroGels MSCA Doctoral Network. Within the European Union’s Horizon Europe research and innovation programme under the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Action (MSCA), University of Helsinki is offering a PhD fellowship for a Doctoral Candidate (DC), starting 1 March 2027 or as soon as possible hereafter. A position is for 36 months and is funded by the FAroGels MSCA Doctoral Network. About the MSCA project FAroGels – Food-Grade Aerogels for Sustainable Food Systems is a Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Network funded by the European Union under Horizon Europe. The network brings together academic and non-academic partners with complementary expertise in food science and technology, materials science, biopolymer science, biotechnology, process engineering, analytical characterization, packaging and sustainable innovation. FAroGels aims to develop, characterize and validate food-grade, bio-based and biodegradable aerogels for sustainable food systems. The network will investigate how these highly porous materials can support more efficient food processing, improved food quality and safety, nutritional and sensory functionality, protection and delivery of bioactive compounds, and sustainable packaging solutions. What We Offer? A full-time doctoral position funded through the FAroGels MSCA Doctoral Network. A fixed-term employment contract for a maximum of 36 months at University of Helsinki. A competitive salary and employment conditions in accordance with the MSCA Doctoral Networks funding provisions, applicable national legislation and the policies of the University of Helsinki. The University of Helsinki offers a comprehensive range of employee benefits and services ( Our University of Helsinki and work culture | University of Helsinki ). Supervision by experienced academic researchers and, where applicable, co-supervisors and/or industrial supervisors. Access to research facilities, infrastructure, training and support services at University of Helsinki and within the FAroGels network. Participation in a structured doctoral training programme including scientific and technical training, transferable skills, career development and innovation-oriented activities. Planned secondments at FAroGels partner organizations, offering complementary scientific, technical and cross-sectoral experience. An international research environment and opportunities to build a professional network across academia and non-academic organizations. Funding Duration and PhD Completion This Doctoral Candidate position is funded by the FAroGels MSCA Doctoral Network for a maximum period of 36 months. Note : Any employment, scholarship or other financial support beyond the 36-month FAroGels funding period is not covered or guaranteed by FAroGels. MSCA Doctoral Network Eligibility Criteria Applicants of any nationality are welcome to apply. At the date of recruitment, applicants must meet the following MSCA Doctoral Network requirements: • Doctoral candidate status : Applicants must not already hold a doctoral degree. Applicants who have successfully defended their doctoral thesis but have not yet been formally awarded the doctoral degree are not eligible. • Mobility rule : Applicants must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in Finland for more than 12 months in the 36 months immediately before the recruitment date. Compulsory national service, short stays such as holidays, and time spent as part of a procedure for obtaining refugee status under the Geneva Convention or EU temporary protection are not taken into account. Compliance is verified before the start of employment, on the basis of the candidate’s passport and, where necessary, other documents. • PhD enrolment : The appointee should either already have the right to pursue a doctoral degree at the University of Helsinki by the start of the appointment or apply for the right and obtain it within the probationary period of six months of their appointment. If the appointee does not already have the right to pursue a doctoral degree at the University of Helsinki, it must be applied for separately, please see How to apply to doctoral programmes | University of Helsinki • Visa and residence permit : a visa or residence/work permit is not required at the application stage and is not a selection criterion. Where needed, it will be arranged after selection and before the start of employment, with the support of the recruiting organisation. Candidates from non-EU countries are equally welcome to apply. The workplace of the position is in Helsinki, Finland. About the open PhD position (DC#14) at University of Helsinki, Finland The selected candidate will be employed by University of Helsinki and will be required to enroll as a PhD student at University of Helsinki, in accordance with the relevant admission and enrolment requirements. The doctoral project will focus on understanding how dietary-fibre aerogels/cryogels can be designed as functional porous and moisture-responsive materials for food applications, including the modulation of texture and mouthfeel. The key scientific challenge is to elucidate how fibre-network organisation, pore-network connectivity, swelling behaviour, pore size distribution, and network integrity govern moisture responsiveness, structural rearrangement, and mechanical response under food-processing and oral conditions. The research will combine material design with multiscale characterisation to establish structure-function relationships. Different approaches will be used to develop dietary-fibre aerogels/cryogels with tailored pore-network architecture and hydration properties. Particular attention will be given to how fibre characteristics and network organisation determine their response to moisture and mechanical stress. The doctoral researcher will gain experience with a range of advanced characterisation and experimental approaches, including Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM), Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM), Confocal Laser Scanning Microscopy (CLSM), phase-enhanced synchrotron X-ray microtomography (XMT), Small-Angle X-ray Scattering (SAXS), Dynamic Vapour Sorption (DVS), and micropipette aspiration. The project will also involve advanced oral tribology using model interfaces such as PDMS-saliva and porcine tongue, in-contact fluorescence imaging, and sensory evaluation in model and real food matrices. These complementary approaches will be used to establish links between material architecture, environmental responsiveness, and functionality in food systems. The PhD researcher will also be expected to develop experience beyond the core laboratory work through research visits and training with collaborating groups, including opportunities in the UK and Germany within the secondments planned for the candidate. These activities will provide experience in advanced characterisation, aerogelation, and the translation of fundamental findings into food applications. This Doctoral Candidate is expected to add an oral-processing dimension to FAroGels, providing next-generation materials that complement sensory control and align with the structural innovations. The position will involve laboratory-based research, data analysis, scientific communication and close collaboration with other FAroGels Doctoral Candidates, supervisors and partner organizations. Main Responsibilities: The Doctoral Candidate will be expected to: Conduct research on material science and food technology. Plan, perform, document and analyse experimental work using relevant laboratory, analytical, processing and/or modelling methods. Participate actively in FAroGels network meetings, workshops, training schools, seminars and transferable-skills activities. Undertake planned secondments at FAroGels partner organisations, as specified for the DC project. Work collaboratively with academic and non-academic partners across the FAroGels network. Prepare scientific reports, project deliverables, presentations, publications and, where relevant, other dissemination or exploitation-related outputs. Requirements Educational Background : Master’s degree (or equivalent) in food technology, materials science, chemistry, biomaterials, or polymer science. Skills : Strong laboratory and analytical skills, with experience in experimental characterisation and quantitative analysis of soft, porous or hydrated materials. We are looking for a curious and proactive researcher with strong scientific thinking, a genuine enthusiasm for learning, and the confidence to explore unfamiliar methods and challenges. The ability and motivation to work across disciplines, learn independently, and pursue new ideas are highly valued. Languages : Proficiency in English (written and spoken) is required. Strong motivation to pursue doctoral research in an international and interdisciplinary environment. Ability to work independently while contributing effectively to collaborative research activities. Good organization, communication and problem-solving skills. Willingness to travel for planned secondments, network meetings and training activities. Commitment to responsible research conduct, inclusion, collegiality and professional development. Salary and Financial Package This position is funded by the FAroGels MSCA Doctoral Network (HORIZON-MSCA-2025-DN-01-01, Grant Agreement No. 101312450). The salary is based on the following monthly MSCA allowances: Living allowance : EUR 4,010, adjusted by the country correction coefficient of the country of the recruiting organisation to ensure equal treatment and comparable purchasing power for all researchers (CCC of Finland = 118.0%). Mobility allowance : EUR 710, contributing to mobility-related costs such as relocation and travel. Family allowance (if applicable): EUR 660, paid for the months in which the Doctoral Candidate has a family during the project, including where family obligations arise after recruitment. ‘Family’ means persons linked to the researcher by marriage (or a relationship with equivalent status recognised by the legislation of the country where it was formalised) or dependent children actually being maintained by the researcher. The allowance is paid in accordance with the rules of the recruiting organisation, and supporting documents may be requested. Please note: the amounts above represent the total employment cost, from which the employer’s and the employee’s compulsory social-security contributions and taxes are deducted in accordance with national law. Unless the recruiting organisation provides additional funding, the gross salary paid to the Doctoral Candidate is therefore lower than these amounts, and the net salary depends on personal circumstances. Application Procedure Your complete online application must be submitted through the University of Helsinki’s recruitment system via the Apply Now button no later than 9 December 2026 (23:59 EET) . In addition to submitting an application through the University of Helsinki recruitment portal, applicants must complete the FAroGels online application form at https://farogels.eu/apply/ and upload the same documents there. Application deadline: 09 December 2026, 23:59 (EET) Expected interview period: January 2027 Evaluation period: February 2027 Expected selection outcome: February 2027 Expected recruitment / start date: March 2027 (earliest) Application Documents Applications must be submitted as one PDF file containing the following documents in English: A detailed curriculum vitae (CV), including education, research experience, technical skills and relevant achievements. Note: It is important to document clearly in your CV that you fulfil the first two MSCA Doctoral Network eligibility criteria (mentioned above), including the doctoral candidate status and mobility rule. A motivation letter explaining your interest in this specific DC position, your relevant background and your suitability for the project. Copies of Bachelor’s and Master’s diplomas and academic transcripts, or evidence that the relevant degree will be completed before the recruitment date. Contact details for two referees and/or reference letters, according to the requirements of University of Helsinki. Proof of English-language proficiency, where required by the University of Helsinki. Concise written responses to the four questions below. These responses are intended to assess the applicant’s scientific understanding, analytical thinking, and potential to work in a multidisciplinary doctoral research project. Relevant references/citations are required. References are not included in the word limit. Aerogels and cryogels: Explain the main differences between aerogels and cryogels, including how their formation processes influence their structure and properties, and why these differences may be relevant to food applications. (Maximum 200 words) Characterisation of porous and soft materials: Describe approaches that can be used to characterise the structure, porosity, hydration/moisture response, and mechanical properties of porous or hydrated soft materials. Explain briefly what information the selected approaches provide. (Maximum 300 words) Critical thinking and problem solving: Describe how you would approach an unexpected, contradictory, or unsuccessful experimental result during your doctoral research. Where possible, provide an example from your previous experience. (Maximum 300 words) Experimental design and scientific reasoning: Consider two fibre-based aerogels with similar composition but different pore network structures, where one produces a smoother mouthfeel than the other. How would you design experiments to investigate the mechanisms responsible for this difference? (Maximum 200 words) Applications that do not include all four responses, or that do not follow the specified requirements, will not be considered. Incomplete applications will not be considered. Selection Process FAroGels follows an open, transparent and merit-based recruitment process in line with the European Charter for Researchers: •  Eligibility check by the FAroGels Project Manager. • Evaluation of the eligible applications by an Interview Committee (primary supervisor, co-supervisor(s) and a representative of a non-academic partner) with a standardised scoring matrix; the three to five best-ranked candidates are shortlisted. • Online interview with the shortlisted candidates. • Approval of the selection by the FAroGels Supervisory Board, after which an offer is made. All applicants are informed of the outcome and receive feedback on request. Equal opportunities and Data Protection University of Helsinki is committed to equal opportunities, diversity and inclusion. Applications from all qualified candidates are welcome, irrespective of gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, ethnic or social origin, nationality, disability, age, religion or belief. Personal data submitted as part of the application will be processed by University of Helsinki exclusively for recruitment purposes and in accordance with applicable data-protection legislation and institutional privacy policies. Contact Information For scientific questions regarding the DC project, please contact: Saman Sabet, Dr, Supervisor Department of Food and Nutrition University of Helsinki saman.sabet@helsinki.fi For questions regarding the application process and/or PhD admission and enrolment , please contact uni-help@helsinki.fi For questions related to the recruitment system , please contact HR Services via recruitment@helsinki.fi About Us The Faculty of Agriculture and Forestry is located on the Viikki campus. The mission of the faculty is to promote the sustainable use of renewable natural resources through scientific research and research-based teaching. We focus on agricultural and forest sciences, food and nutrition, microbiology, as well as on economics and management. We cover the full natural resource chain from farm to fork, the entire field of forest sciences from soil chemistry to marketing, as well as the environmental effects of related activities. Graduates from the Faculty of Agriculture and Forestry are versatile experts in agriculture and food and forest sectors. www.helsinki.fi/en/faculty-of-agriculture-and-forestry Why Finland and Helsinki Why Finland is a great place to live and work: https://www.helsinki.fi/en/about-us/careers/why-finland 10 reasons to work in Helsinki: https://www.helsinki.fi/en/about-us/careers/why-helsinki Support for international staff: https://www.helsinki.fi/en/about-us/careers/welcome International House Helsinki: https://ihhelsinki.fi Welcome to Helsinki: https://welcome.hel.fi/ A diverse and equitable study and work culture is important to us. That is why we do our best to promote an inclusive university community. We encourage all qualified applicants from diverse backgrounds to apply for our positions. Click this link to read about accessibility and inclusivity at our University. JOIN US TO BUILD A BETTER WORLD – TOGETHER! #HelsinkiUniCareers Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 9.12.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Agriculture and Forestry Sub-unit: Department of Food and Nutrition ID: 5255 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Joi"
    },
    "https://jobs.helsinki.fi/job/Helsinki-Doctoral-Researcher-%28MSCA%29%2C-Exploiting-Migration-to-Influence-and-Destabilise-Societies/1374784157/": {
      "title": "Doctoral Researcher (MSCA), Exploiting Migration to Influence and Destabilise Societies",
      "description": "Doctoral Researcher (MSCA), Exploiting Migration to Influence and Destabilise Societies Job Details | University of Helsinki We use cookies to offer you the best possible website experience. Your cookie preferences will be stored in your browser’s local storage. This includes cookies necessary for the website's operation. Additionally, you can freely decide and change any time whether you accept cookies or choose to opt out of cookies to improve the website's performance, as well as cookies used to display content tailored to your interests. Your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer may be impacted if you do not accept all cookies. Modify Cookie Preferences Accept All Cookies Skip to main content View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login View all jobs View by category Professors and assistant / associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions Careers Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login VIEW ALL JOBS VIEW BY CATEGORY Professors, assistant and associate professors Teaching and research positions Leadership, specialist and support positions CAREERS Language English Suomi Svenska Profile Employee Login Search by title, unit or a word Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Doctoral Researcher (MSCA), Exploiting Migration to Influence and Destabilise Societies MSCA Doctoral Network INFLUENCE “Legacies, Interdependencies, Power: Russian Influences in the European Periphery” invites applications for the position of DOCTORAL RESEARCHER, Exploiting Migration to Influence and Destabilise Societies in Europe’s Eastern Periphery for a full-time position for three years from October 2027. Funded by the Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions (MSCA) Doctoral Networks programme of Horizon Europe, INFLUENCE brings together 5 universities across Europe to train a new generation of scholars and practitioners working on the study of Russian influence in Europe. About INFLUENCE Russian influence in Europe is frequently discussed in public debate, yet its mechanisms remain poorly understood. Investigating influence through the dimensions of Legacies, Interdependencies and Power, the Doctoral Network combines expertise from political science, international relations, area studies, sociology, history, media studies, security studies and political economy. Through interdisciplinary research, advanced methodological training and engagement with policy stakeholders, INFLUENCE seeks to advance scholarly and policy understanding of Russian influence in contemporary Europe. Partner Institutions: Vilnius University (Lithuania); University of Glasgow (United Kingdom); University of Helsinki (Finland); University of Sarajevo (Bosnia and Herzegovina); University of Warsaw (Poland). Doctoral candidates will benefit from joint supervision, intersectoral secondments, strong transferable skills development, and a bridge between academic research and policy practice. Secondments for at least 6 months are integral component of the INFLUENCE Doctoral Programme with mandatory research stays at academic and non-academic partner institutions. Position at the University of Helsinki: Exploiting Migration to Influence and Destabilise Societies in Europe’s Eastern Periphery Description: This project examines how Russia exploits societal divisions over migration as part of its broader strategy to destabilise and influence European societies. Russian state-controlled media and social media platforms have been instrumental in disseminating conflicting narratives, accusing the West of both Western-centric colonialism and overly liberal migration policies. These narratives are not only targeted at Russian-speaking populations through Russian-language media but are also circulated across Europe through social media accounts, bots, and fake news sites in various European languages. This project will analyse how these narratives are constructed, disseminated, and adjusted to exploit specific societal vulnerabilities to fuel polarisation, undermine social cohesion, and erode trust in democratic institutions. By combining discourse analysis and media monitoring with case studies of significant migration-related events, the research will assess how migration narratives are tailored to specific European contexts and their role in creating political fragmentation and fostering extremist ideologies. Additionally, the project will evaluate the interplay between domestic migration policies in European states and the narratives promoted by Russia, highlighting vulnerabilities that facilitate external interference. What We Offer 36‑month full-time EU MSCA contract and fully funded doctoral position Salary, pension and terms of employment are in accordance with the requirements from the European Commission related to the MSCA programme. The salary (monthly living allowance) is 3 639,85 € per month before taxes. Additionally, a monthly mobility allowance 546,15 € per month before taxes will be paid. If applicable, a conditional monthly family allowance of 507,69 € per month before taxes is paid. Joint international supervision Advanced research, methods and transferable skills training Research visits and intersectoral secondments Professional development opportunities and career guidance Standard Finnish pension benefits and occupational health care are provided for University of Helsinki employees. Read more about the employee benefits at the University of Helsinki and why the University of Helsinki is an excellent career choice: https://www.helsinki.fi/en/about-us/careers . Please also see about relocation support: https://www.helsinki.fi/en/about-us/careers/welcome-finland-information-arriving-staff Eligibility Criteria Applicants must hold a relevant Master’s degree or equivalent which formally entitles them to start a doctorate at the time of application Applicants cannot have been awarded a doctoral degree. Applicants can be of any nationality; however, they must comply with the mobility rule: must not have resided or carried out their main activity (work, studies, etc.) in the country of the recruiting organisation for more than 12 months in the 36 months immediately before their recruitment date. All positions require excellent English-language skills, some projects may particularly benefit from knowledge of Russian and/or languages spoken in the Baltic States and the Western Balkans. Successful candidates have to be ready to work in a team, travel to conferences and network training events, and to take part in an academic and non-academic secondments abroad. The appointee must obtain the right to pursue a doctoral degree at the University of Helsinki and approval by a suitable doctoral programme by the end of the standard 6-month probationary period of the employment, unless having already existing study right. The research plan and other application material will be prepared together with the PhD supervisor(s) in the beginning of the employment period. For further information on eligibility criteria and on Marie Skłodowska-Curie Actions Doctoral Networks in general, please refer to: https://marie-sklodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/actions/doctoral-networks General Selection Process INFLUENCE is committed to transparent, merit-based and inclusive recruitment in line with: the European Charter for Researchers: https://euraxess.ec.europa.eu/hrexcellenceaward/european-charter-researchers ; the principles of the Code of Conduct for the Recruitment of Researchers: https://www.euraxess.de/sites/default/files/brochures/C%26C_EN.pdf ; the MSCA Guidelines on Supervision: https://marie-sklodowska-curie-actions.ec.europa.eu/about-msca/guidelines-on-supervision Recruitment will take place through an open, international, and competitive selection procedure conducted in two stages. In the first stage, applications will be evaluated on the basis of academic excellence, research potential, methodological and disciplinary preparedness, motivation, relevant research experience, the quality and feasibility of the proposed research, and its fit with the project and available supervisory expertise. Candidates who demonstrate the strongest overall profiles will be shortlisted and invited to an interview in the second stage. The interview will provide an opportunity to further assess candidates’ research aptitude, motivation, understanding of the proposed research, ability to discuss and develop their ideas, and overall suitability for the doctoral project. Key Dates Call opens: 12 October 2026 Application deadline: 15 December 2026 Shortlisted of Candidates prepared: February 2027 Interviews with Shortlisted Candidates: February – March 2027 Results communicated: by 30 April 2027 Employment commences: 1 October 2027 How to apply Applicants should be prepared to submit the following documents as PDF-files: Cover letter Prospective research proposal outlining your interest in the specific project Curriculum Vitae Degree certificates and academic transcripts Please submit your application to the University of Helsinki Recruitment System via the Apply Now button (or Employee login). Application deadline is on the 15 December 2026 at 23:59 (Europe/Helsinki time). Equality, Diversity and Inclusion The network welcomes applications from candidates of all backgrounds and is committed to an inclusive and equitable research environment. Further Information For enquiries about the position, please contact Ira Jänis-Isokangas, ira.janis-isokangas@helsinki.fi. For enquiries about the recruitment system, please contact recruitment@helsinki.fi (please indicate the title of the call in the email heading). Data Protection and Consent Notice By applying for this position, applicants consent to circulate their application materials and personal data among authorised INFLUENCE consortium members involved in the recruitment process. All data provided by the applicants will be processed in accordance with the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR, EU 2016/679) and used solely for the purpose of selecting doctoral candidates. Do you have a helsinki.fi username and a valid employment contract, a grant researcher's contract, a visiting researcher's or visiting professor's contract at the University of Helsinki? Log in here to apply to our open positions. Employee Login Open position Due Date: 15.12.2026 (23.59 Europe/Helsinki time) Unit: Faculty of Humanities Sub-unit: Aleksanteri Institute ID: 5264 Working for the world Science has the power to change the world. Join one of the best research universities in the world – an international community united through bold thinking, science, research, and learning, with over 30,000 students and 8,000 employees. We are defined by our multidisciplinary approach. This, combined with a dedication for cross-border collaboration, gives us our potential to find real solutions and build hope for a better, more sustainable future. We are united by our values – truth, bildung, freedom, and inclusivity – as well as our passion for work that truly matters. Join us: work for the future – work for the world. Any questions? See our recruitment system FAQ Apply now » Find similar jobs: Teaching and research positions Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. CONTACT Data protection Contact us WHY US? Why University of Helsinki Why Finland CAREER SITES Academic careers Expert careers FACULTIES AND UNITS Flamma intranet Faculties and Units © University of Helsinki 2022 × Cookie Consent Manager When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. Because we respect your